{
  "image_name": "68da7a9264a2dacb0bf59caef2d962f7.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how photoexcited carriers behave in perovskite materials and at their interfaces. At the top, it sketches the basic sequence after light absorption: a photon creates an electron–hole pair, the carriers quickly thermalize, and then recombine through several competing pathways. These include radiative emission, defect-assisted nonradiative recombination through trap states, and Auger recombination, shown in both direct and phonon-assisted indirect forms.\n\nThe middle row focuses on interfacial energetics between perovskite and neighboring transport layers. Four interface “types” illustrate different alignments of conduction/valence bands with molecular levels such as LUMO and HOMO, and show how defects near the junction can either promote charge extraction or act as recombination centers. The diagrams emphasize that unfavorable offsets or interfacial trap states can strongly alter carrier flow across the boundary.\n\nThe bottom row compares two conceptual pictures of trap-mediated recombination in a band-bending region. In the hopping model, carriers move through localized defect states toward recombination and photon loss. In the multiple-trapping model, carriers are repeatedly captured, escape, and recaptured among traps before recombining. Overall, the figure highlights how defects, band alignment, and trap dynamics govern charge transport and recombination losses in perovskite optoelectronic devices.",
